import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class StringHasher {
    private static final int BUFFER_SIZE = 1024;
    private static final ThreadLocal<MessageDigest> digester = new ThreadLocal<MessageDigest>() {
        @Override
        protected MessageDigest initialValue() {
            return MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
        }
    };

    public List<String> hashStrings(List<String> strings) {
        if (strings == null || strings.isEmpty()) {
            return Collections.emptyList();
        }

        List<String> hashes = new ArrayList<>();
        for (String string : strings) {
            if (string != null && !string.isEmpty()) {
                try {
                    byte[] bytes = string.getBytes("UTF-8");
                    digester.get().reset();
                    digester.get().update(bytes);
                    hashes.add(new String(digester.get().digest()));
                } catch (UnsupportedEncodingException e) {
                    // handle invalid UTF-8 encoding gracefully
                }
            }
        }

        return hashes;
    }
}
```
This code uses a `ThreadLocal` variable to store the `MessageDigest` instance, which allows for efficient reuse of the same instance across multiple threads. The `hashStrings` method first checks if the input list is null or empty, and returns an empty list in that case. It then iterates over each string in the input list, and uses a try-catch block to handle any invalid UTF-8 encoding errors gracefully. If the string is not null or empty, it gets converted to bytes using the "UTF-8" charset, and the `MessageDigest` instance is reset before updating it with the byte array. Finally, the resulting hash value is added to the output list.']

