The function first checks for invalid pointers and out-of-bounds access, and returns NULL if any of these conditions are met. It then allocates a new state list dynamically using `malloc` and populates it with states that meet the condition by iterating over each state in the FSA and checking its label and transition targets. If a state meets the condition, it is added to the new state list.

The function also ensures that the 'next' pointer of the last state in the current state list points to the newly created state list upon successful allocation. This is done by setting `fsa->states[last_state].next = new_list` if the last state is not the last state in the FSA.

The function optimizes memory usage by reusing existing state objects if possible, but ensuring that the state list remains logically distinct. This is done by checking each state in the FSA and its transition targets to see if any of them are already present in the new state list. If a state or its transition target is already present, it is not added again to the new state list.

