// Function to initialize an AES-256-CBC cipher with a given password
EVP_CIPHER_CTX* init_cipher(const char* password) {
    // Check if the OpenSSL library is available
    if (!OPENSSL_init_crypto(OPENSSL_INIT_LOAD_CRYPTO_STRINGS, NULL)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: OpenSSL library not available\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Set the IV length and key length based on design choices
    int iv_len = 16; // 128 bits
    int key_len = 32; // 256 bits

    // Derive the encryption key from the password using a secure but efficient hashing algorithm
    unsigned char key[key_len];
    EVP_BytesToKey(EVP_aes_256_cbc(), EVP_sha256(), iv_len, (unsigned char*)password, strlen(password), 1, key, NULL);

    // Initialize the AES-256-CBC cipher with the derived key and IV
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX* ctx = EVP_CIPHER_CTX_new();
    if (!EVP_EncryptInit_ex(ctx, EVP_aes_256_cbc(), NULL, key, iv)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Failed to initialize AES-256-CBC cipher\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Enable the cipher and return a pointer to the cipher context
    if (!EVP_EncryptEnable(ctx)) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: Failed to enable AES-256-CBC cipher\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Clean up and disable the cipher when it is no longer needed
    EVP_CIPHER_CTX_free(ctx);
}
```
This function first checks if the OpenSSL library is available, and then sets the IV length and key length based on design choices. It then derives the encryption key from the password using a secure but efficient hashing algorithm, and initializes the AES-256-CBC cipher with the derived key and IV. The function enables the cipher and returns a pointer to the cipher context. Finally, it provides a function to clean up and disable the cipher when it is no longer needed.

Note that this code assumes that the OpenSSL library is available at runtime, and that the password is long enough to generate a secure key. If the OpenSSL library is not available or if the password is too short, the function will return an error message. Additionally, this code uses the SHA-256 hash algorithm for key derivation, which provides a high level of security but may have some performance overhead compared to other hashing algorithms.']

