// Define the structure for a test configuration
typedef struct LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T {
    int channelBandwidth;
    int centerFrequency;
    int rfModulationType;
} LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T;

// Define the list of test configurations
static LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T *testConfigList = NULL;

// Define the mutex for synchronizing access to the list
static pthread_mutex_t listMutex = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;

// Function to add a new test configuration to the list
void addTestConfiguration(int channelBandwidth, int centerFrequency, int rfModulationType) {
    // Acquire the mutex for synchronizing access to the list
    pthread_mutex_lock(&listMutex);

    // Check if the test configuration is already in the list
    LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T *config = findTestConfiguration(channelBandwidth, centerFrequency, rfModulationType);
    if (config != NULL) {
        // If the configuration is already in the list, log an appropriate message and return
        printf("Duplicate test configuration detected: %d MHz, %s modulation\n", centerFrequency, getRFModulationName(rfModulationType));
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
        return;
    }

    // Allocate memory for the new test configuration structure
    LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T *newConfig = malloc(sizeof(LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T));
    if (newConfig == NULL) {
        // If allocation failed, log an error message and return
        printf("Failed to allocate memory for new test configuration\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
        return;
    }

    // Initialize the new test configuration structure
    newConfig->channelBandwidth = channelBandwidth;
    newConfig->centerFrequency = centerFrequency;
    newConfig->rfModulationType = rfModulationType;

    // Add the new test configuration to the list
    if (testConfigList == NULL) {
        // If the list is empty, set the new configuration as the only element
        testConfigList = newConfig;
    } else {
        // Otherwise, add the new configuration to the end of the list
        LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T *lastConfig = testConfigList;
        while (lastConfig->next != NULL) {
            lastConfig = lastConfig->next;
        }
        lastConfig->next = newConfig;
    }

    // Release the mutex for synchronizing access to the list
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
}

// Function to find a test configuration in the list by its channel bandwidth, center frequency, and RF modulation type
LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T *findTestConfiguration(int channelBandwidth, int centerFrequency, int rfModulationType) {
    // Acquire the mutex for synchronizing access to the list
    pthread_mutex_lock(&listMutex);

    // Iterate through the list of test configurations until a matching configuration is found or the end of the list is reached
    LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T *config = testConfigList;
    while (config != NULL) {
        if (config->channelBandwidth == channelBandwidth && config->centerFrequency == centerFrequency && config->rfModulationType == rfModulationType) {
            // If a matching configuration is found, release the mutex and return it
            pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
            return config;
        }
        config = config->next;
    }

    // If no matching configuration was found, release the mutex and return NULL
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
    return NULL;
}

// Function to export the test configurations to a CSV file
void exportTestConfigurations(const char *filename) {
    // Acquire the mutex for synchronizing access to the list
    pthread_mutex_lock(&listMutex);

    // Open the output file in append mode
    FILE *outputFile = fopen(filename, "a");
    if (outputFile == NULL) {
        // If opening the file failed, log an error message and return
        printf("Failed to open output file for exporting test configurations\n");
        pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
        return;
    }

    // Iterate through the list of test configurations and write each configuration to the output file
    LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T *config = testConfigList;
    while (config != NULL) {
        fprintf(outputFile, "%d,%d,%s\n", config->channelBandwidth, config->centerFrequency, getRFModulationName(config->rfModulationType));
        config = config->next;
    }

    // Close the output file and release the mutex for synchronizing access to the list
    fclose(outputFile);
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&listMutex);
}
```
In this implementation, we use a linked list of `LTE_PHY_TEST_CONFIG_PDU_T` structures to store the test configurations. Each structure contains the channel bandwidth, center frequency, and RF modulation type for a single test configuration. We use a mutex to synchronize access to the list, as multiple threads may attempt to add or remove test configurations simultaneously.

To handle duplicates in the list, we check if a matching configuration already exists before adding a new one. If a duplicate is detected, we log an appropriate message and return without modifying the list. We also use a mutex to protect the `findTestConfiguration` function, which is used to search for a specific test configuration in the list.

To export the test configurations to a CSV file, we use the `exportTestConfigurations` function. This function opens the output file in append mode and iterates through the list of test configurations, writing each configuration to the file using the `fprintf` function. We use a mutex to synchronize access to the list during this process.

In terms of performance, we prioritize data consistency over speed in case of I/O failures. This means that we may experience longer latency during high-throughput scenarios if file operations are frequently failing due to external dependencies such as file access permissions or network connectivity issues. However, this design choice allows us to gracefully handle these failures without compromising system stability.

In terms of memory usage, we use a linked list for efficient dynamic memory management. This approach allows us to add and remove test configurations efficiently without incurring excessive resource consumption. We also use the `malloc` function to allocate memory for new test configuration structures, which ensures that memory is allocated safely and robustly.

Overall, this implementation balances memory usage against performance by using a linked list for efficient dynamic memory management and prioritizing data consistency over speed in case of I/O failures.']

