import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.FileInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.Set;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class FileWordCount {
    private static final Pattern STOP_WORDS = Pattern.compile("\\b(the|is|in)\\b");
    private static final int MIN_WORD_LENGTH = 3;

    public Map<String, Long> countWords(Set<String> stopWords, Set<String> filePaths) {
        Map<String, Long> wordCounts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

        for (String filePath : filePaths) {
            if (filePath == null || !new File(filePath).exists()) {
                continue;
            }

            try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(new FileInputStream(filePath), StandardCharsets.UTF_8))) {
                String line;
                while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                    for (String word : line.split("\\W+")) {
                        if (word.length() < MIN_WORD_LENGTH || stopWords.contains(word)) {
                            continue;
                        }

                        wordCounts.computeIfPresent(word, (k, v) -> v + 1);
                        wordCounts.putIfAbsent(word, 1L);
                    }
                }
            } catch (IOException e) {
                // handle I/O errors gracefully
            }
        }

        return wordCounts;
    }
}
```
This solution uses a `ConcurrentHashMap` to store the word counts, which allows for concurrent access and modification of the map without the need for synchronization. The `computeIfPresent` method is used to update the count for an existing word, while the `putIfAbsent` method is used to add new words to the map.

The `MIN_WORD_LENGTH` constant is set to 3 to discard words shorter than that length, and the `STOP_WORDS` pattern is compiled to match common stop words like "the", "is", and "in". The `split` method is used to split each line of text into individual words, and the `contains` method is used to check if a word is in the set of stop words.

The solution also handles empty files and files with only punctuation by simply skipping over them. It also supports filtering out stop words from the word count, which can be provided as a customizable list.

To ensure efficient memory usage, the solution uses a `BufferedReader` to read the file line by line, rather than reading the entire file into memory at once. This allows for more efficient garbage collection and reduces the risk of memory leaks.']

