After creating all of the new states, it checks if the last state in the provided list is not -1 (indicating that no specific last state was provided). If this is the case, it sets the 'next' pointer of the last state in the provided list to point to the newly created list.

Finally, the function returns a pointer to the tail of the newly created list, which is the last state in the list. This allows the caller to access the new states that were added to the list.

It's important to note that this function assumes that the provided state list is not empty and that the FSA is fully initialized. If either of these conditions are not true, the function will return NULL to indicate an error."]

