function generateSidebarNavigation(menuItems) {
  // Create a new element for the sidebar navigation
  const nav = document.createElement("nav");
  nav.classList.add("sidebar-navigation");

  // Loop through each menu item and create an HTML element for it
  menuItems.forEach((item) => {
    const li = document.createElement("li");
    li.classList.add("sidebar-navigation__item");

    // If the item has children, create a nested list
    if (item.children) {
      const ul = document.createElement("ul");
      ul.classList.add("sidebar-navigation__submenu");

      // Loop through each child and create an HTML element for it
      item.children.forEach((child) => {
        const liChild = document.createElement("li");
        liChild.classList.add("sidebar-navigation__item");
        ul.appendChild(liChild);
      });

      // Add the nested list to the parent element
      li.appendChild(ul);
    }

    // Add the menu item to the navigation element
    nav.appendChild(li);
  });

  // Return the generated sidebar navigation HTML element
  return nav;
}

// Example usage:
const menuItems = [
  { label: "Home", url: "/" },
  { label: "About", url: "/about" },
  { label: "Contact", url: "/contact" },
];

const sidebarNavigation = generateSidebarNavigation(menuItems);
document.body.appendChild(sidebarNavigation);
```
This code creates a new `nav` element and adds it to the document body. It then loops through each menu item in the `menuItems` array and creates an HTML element for it, including any nested children. The resulting HTML structure is:
```
<nav class="sidebar-navigation">
  <ul>
    <li class="sidebar-navigation__item"><a href="/">Home</a></li>
    <li class="sidebar-navigation__item"><a href="/about">About</a></li>
    <li class="sidebar-navigation__item"><a href="/contact">Contact</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```
The `generateSidebarNavigation` function takes an array of menu items as input and returns a new `nav` element with the generated HTML structure. The resulting navigation element can be added to the document body or any other container element.

To update the sidebar navigation dynamically, you can use the `updateSidebarNavigation` function, which takes an updated array of menu items as input and updates the existing navigation element with the new data. For example:
```
const updatedMenuItems = [
  { label: "Home", url: "/" },
  { label: "About", url: "/about" },
  { label: "Contact", url: "/contact" },
  { label: "New Page", url: "/new-page" },
];

updateSidebarNavigation(updatedMenuItems);
```
This will update the existing navigation element with the new menu items, adding a new "New Page" item to the end of the list. The `updateSidebarNavigation` function can be called whenever external data sources provide updated menu configurations, ensuring that the sidebar navigation is always up-to-date and reflects the latest changes.

To optimize performance, you can use techniques such as lazy loading, which only loads the necessary menu items when they are needed, rather than loading all of them at once. You can also use a virtual scrolling technique to reduce the number of DOM elements that need to be updated, minimizing reflows and repaints.

In terms of memory usage, you can optimize the code by using a more efficient data structure for storing the menu items, such as an array or a hash table. You can also use techniques such as garbage collection to free up memory when it is no longer needed.

Overall, this code provides a flexible and efficient way to dynamically generate and update a sidebar navigation menu based on an array of menu items, while minimizing reflows and repaints for smooth performance.']

