The program also includes error handling for invalid command line arguments, missing required options, and I/O errors. It uses the `perror` function to display error messages when an error occurs.

To handle concurrent access from multiple threads when processing multiple files simultaneously, the program uses a mutex to synchronize access to the shared data structures. It also uses a condition variable to signal when a thread is waiting for a mutex and when it has acquired it.

The program is designed to be robust against large files by reading them in chunks rather than loading the entire file into memory. It also includes a constraint that the program must avoid using more than 1GB of RAM at any point during execution. To achieve this, it uses a fixed-size buffer to read the input file in chunks, and it allocates memory for each chunk dynamically.

The program allows users to specify a custom progress update interval in seconds, which can affect the performance and responsiveness of the program. It uses the `sleep` function to pause execution for a specified number of seconds between updates.

