If rate limiting is enabled, the function checks if the logging interval has elapsed since the last logged message, and if not, it returns without logging anything. If the logging interval has elapsed or rate limiting is disabled, it updates the `last_logged_at` variable with the current time in milliseconds and logs the message according to the specified verbosity level.

The function also includes a mechanism for suppressing certain types of log messages based on their content or metadata using the `LOG_SUPPRESSION` environment variable. If this variable is set, the function checks if the message contains the specified suppression string, and if so, it returns without logging anything.

Finally, the function is thread-safe by acquiring a mutex before logging any messages to prevent data corruption when multiple threads try to log messages simultaneously. It also includes rate limiting to control the frequency of log messages, ensuring that no more than 50 messages per second are logged under any circumstances.

